q077.page_content:
Support vector machine > Extensions > Transductive support vector machines
Transductive support vector machines extend SVMs in that they could also treat partially labeled data in semi-supervised learning by following the principles of transduction. Here, in addition to the training set {\mathcal {D}}, the learner is also given a set {\mathcal {D}}^{\star }=\{\mathbf {x} _{i}^{\star }\mid \mathbf {x} _{i}^{\star }\in \mathbb {R} ^{p}\}_{i=1}^{k}
of test examples to be classified. Formally, a transductive support vector machine is defined by the following primal optimization problem:
Minimize (in \mathbf {w} ,b,\mathbf {y} ^{\star }) {\frac {1}{2}}\|\mathbf {w} \|^{2}
subject to (for any i=1,\dots ,n and any j=1,\dots ,k) {\begin{aligned}&y_{i}(\mathbf {w} \cdot \mathbf {x} _{i}-b)\geq 1,\\&y_{j}^{\star }(\mathbf {w} \cdot \mathbf {x} _{j}^{\star }-b)\geq 1,\end{aligned}}
and y_{j}^{\star }\in \{-1,1\}.
Transductive support vector machines were introduced by Vladimir N. Vapnik in 1998.

